    "source": "cboe-options — the chain's shape is transcribed from the Cboe AAPL option-chain capture: two monthly expiries (2026-10-16 and 2026-12-18, both third Fridays), $5 strikes from 200 to 265 around a 232.50 spot, two-sided markets on both sides of every strike, and the vendor's own IV column. WP-02 must not open fixtures/providers/raw/ or go through providers/replayStore.ts (WORKPLAN L471-482), so the levels are written out literally here; the same fit over the *replayed* chain is WP-11's integration test. The quotes are one self-consistent arbitrage-free snapshot: each side's mid is Black-76 on the parity forward F = S*e^((r-q)T) at that strike's quoted IV, then rounded to the penny inside the quoted spread, so the fit sees exactly the half-cent of mid rounding a real chain carries and nothing else. Expected values, every one of them derived without running the SVI fit: (1) frontT = 31/365 = 0.08493150684931507 and backT = 94/365 = 0.25753424657534246 are the ACT/365F fractions from 2026-09-15 (tolerance 1e-12, double rounding only). (2) frontForward = 232.50*e^((0.0412-0.0044)*31/365) = 233.22781076 and backForward = 232.50*e^((0.0412-0.0044)*94/365) = 234.71393747; the put-call-parity regression over the fourteen two-sided strikes must recover them, and the 0.01 tolerance is the forward error half a cent of mid rounding leaves on a $233 forward (the regression in fact lands 0.0007 and 0.0002 away). (3) frontAtmIv = 0.26 and backAtmIv = 0.28 are the chain's own quoted IV column interpolated to the forward — front 0.2670 at K=230 and 0.2565 at K=235 give 0.2602 at F=233.228, back 0.2859 and 0.2797 give 0.2801 at F=234.714 — tolerated at 1e-3, the gap between a straight-line interpolation of the quoted column and the fitted smile. (4) nQuotes = 28 = 2 expiries x 14 strikes, every strike usable; nSlices = 2. (5) arbitrageFree = true, butterflyArbitrage = false, calendarArbitrage = false are structural, not measured: a chain quoted from one raw-SVI slice per expiry whose parameters satisfy a + b*sigma*sqrt(1-rho^2) > 0 and whose total variance rises with maturity at every log-moneyness has a positive risk-neutral density and no calendar spread with a negative price."
